1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. d4 Nf6 5. dxc5 Na6 6. a3 Nxc5 7. b4 Nce4 8. Nd4 d6 9. f3 Ng5 10. Nc3 O-O 11. e4 Ne6 {The unusual opening has resolved to a Maroczy Bind structure where White has postponed his king’s side development in favour of space-gaining pawn thrusts on the opposite flank. If White is given time to consolidate, then his territorial supremacy will assume real importance, so Black must open lines swiftly if he is to exploit his superior development.} 12. Be3 a5 13. Rc1 axb4 14. axb4 Nh5 15. Nd5 ({A misguided attempt to cover his sensi­tive dark squares, but even after} 15. Be2 {Black would have a good game, e.g.} Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bh6 $1 17. Rc2 e5 18. Bf2 Qg5 19. g3 ({or} 19. g4 Nf4 20. h4 Qe7 $1) 19... Bh3 20. Nd5 Ra3) 15... Nxd4 16. Bxd4 Bxd4 17. Qxd4 e6 18. Nc3 Qh4+ 19. Qf2 Qg5 20. Qd2 Qh4+ 21. Qf2 Qf6 22. g3 {} Ra1 $1 {A straight right! Bob reels and staggers on, but the fight could almost have been stopped.} 23. Qd2 Rxc1+ 24. Qxc1 Qxf3 25. Rg1 Qf6 26. Be2 Qe5 27. Bxh5 Qxh5 28. Rg2 Bd7 29. Qf4 e5 30. Qd2 Be6 31. Nd5 Bxd5 32. exd5 Ra8 33. Qc3 Qf5 34. Rd2 Qb1+ 35. Kf2 Ra1 36. Re2 Qg1+ 37. Kf3 f5 $1 38. Qb2 Rf1+ 39. Rf2 Qh1+ {Source: bulletin; CHESS, July 1974, Vol.39/701-2, p292} 0-1

