1. Nf3 b6 2. e4 Bb7 3. Nc3 e6 4. d4 Bb4 5. Bd3 Ne7 6. O-O Bxc3 7. bxc3 d6 8. a4 a5 9. Ne1 (9. Ng5 h6 10. Nh3 {followed by piece play on the king�s side is a better plan.}) 9... O-O 10. f4 f5 11. Qe2 Qd7 (11... Nd7 {intending 12...Nf6 would give Black less cause for later regret.}) 12. Nf3 Nbc6 13. exf5 exf5 14. Bc4+ Kh8 15. Re1 Ng8 16. Be6 Qe8 17. d5 Nd8 18. Nd4 Nxe6 19. Nxe6 Rf7 20. c4 Re7 21. Bb2 Qg6 $2 22. Qd3 $2 (22. Ra3 $1) 22... Rae8 23. Re3 Rxe6 24. Rg3 Re4 $4 (24... Qxg3 25. Qxg3 R6e7 26. Bc3 $18 {Stockfish 17}) 25. Rxg6 {Only now does Black realise that Nh6 fails to parry the impending Qh3+. Simply 24...Qxg3 would have left Black with an eminently playable position. Sources: bulletin; CHESS, July 1974, Vol.39/701-2, p293; Basman scoresheet (https://michael-basman-legacy.github.io/scoresheets/1974/Athenaeum%206-7-74.pdf)} 1-0

